1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:23,120
Okay, this is the latest episode of Concord Storytellers, take two.

2
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:28,080
We just tried recording and one of the microphones decided not to work, so we are back at it.

3
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:32,840
And I have today, again, a special guest.

4
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:38,320
I was saying before, I'm waiting to have just sort of an average Joe guest, but today, this

5
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:39,760
does not qualify.

6
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:42,400
I have Amity Wilczek here.

7
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:44,080
Amity, please let us know.

8
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:49,440
Give us your 30,000 foot view of the life and times and we can go on from there.

9
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:50,440
Sure.

10
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:51,440
My name is Amity Wilczek.

11
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:57,080
As you mentioned, some people know me also as Mickey Wilczek, my middle name, Mickalena,

12
00:00:57,080 --> 00:01:03,680
and for my very strong minded and strong tempered, impressive, great grandmother, Italian grandmother,

13
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:05,040
Mickalena Kona.

14
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:12,600
I am trained as an evolutionary ecologist and had a career in both research and teaching

15
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:18,720
as an evolutionary ecologist, transitioned a few years ago to more applied work.

16
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:23,880
I now work for the federal government, specifically for at the Volpe Center, which is part of

17
00:01:23,880 --> 00:01:30,080
the Department of Transportation doing climate analysis and policy work.

18
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:31,160
Awesome.

19
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:34,600
I think that, well, a few things.

20
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:37,400
One, you are my number one draft pick in life.

21
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:42,280
If the apocalypse hits and we have to live off the land to survive, I'm coming straight

22
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:43,280
to you.

23
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:44,280
I want to say that.

24
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:48,600
Because you've had a really, I think that you must have a really hard time doing two

25
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:52,040
truths and one lie when you're doing social interactions.

26
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:57,800
Because you can tell three truths and zero lies and they're all sound like lies.

27
00:01:57,800 --> 00:01:59,960
Can you think of the top three coolest things you've done?

28
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:00,960
I have my own list.

29
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:03,200
I want to see how much it coincides.

30
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:05,920
The top three, if you did two truths and one lie, but they were all truths, what would

31
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:06,920
they be?

32
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:07,920
Okay.

33
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:08,920
So a few truths about myself.

34
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:14,840
It's not that unusual that you have to say, have to come up with something, tell us something

35
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:19,080
about yourself that no one else knows or that no one in this group knows.

36
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:28,120
So one of the things I often say is that I have worked as a sheep midwife on three continents.

37
00:02:28,120 --> 00:02:30,760
So that is true.

38
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:36,960
Another thing I have done is for over the span of about 10 to 15 years, I was a natural

39
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:43,560
history lecturer on tours of various locations, particularly islands.

40
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:49,200
And in fact, two different trips that were islands of the world by private jet.

41
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:54,560
So I've been to a lot of islands that are off the beaten path in my lifespan and I hope

42
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:55,560
to go to many more.

43
00:02:55,560 --> 00:03:04,440
And let's see, what is a good third one?

44
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:05,720
Let's start with those two for now.

45
00:03:05,720 --> 00:03:06,720
Okay.

46
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:07,720
Maybe we'll get to the third one.

47
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:08,720
Yeah.

48
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:10,680
I think, well, I think we're going to do a deep dive into deep springs.

49
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:11,680
Yeah.

50
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:15,760
I think there's probably about 30 things from there from life at deep springs that could

51
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:17,920
probably qualify.

52
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:21,560
Before we jump into that, can you talk about your ties to Concord?

53
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:22,560
My ties to Concord.

54
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:28,160
So I moved here almost exactly four years ago now and it's been wonderful.

55
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:34,200
This was not my first time living in Concord, however, fairly randomly I ended up being

56
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:38,000
a high school student for all four years of high school at Concord Academy.

57
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,960
My first year I was a day student taking the train every day from Cambridge.

58
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:47,200
And then the last three years my parents moved away.

59
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:50,840
I think they were hoping I was going to move with them, but I kind of secretly applied

60
00:03:50,840 --> 00:03:52,880
to be a boarding student.

61
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:53,880
I had other ideas.

62
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:56,200
We moved around a lot and I liked Concord.

63
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:59,720
I liked Concord Academy a lot and I wanted to stay.

64
00:03:59,720 --> 00:04:05,160
And they were kind enough to recognize my intention there and to honor it.

65
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:07,600
So I then was a boarding student for three years.

66
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:08,600
That's cool.

67
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:09,600
And loved it then.

68
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:14,400
I walked around many, many a weekend.

69
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:19,720
I would spend an entire day walking through Concord, which was fun then.

70
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:20,720
And now it's spectacular.

71
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:25,160
There are so many more established trails than there were when I was a high school student

72
00:04:25,160 --> 00:04:26,160
here.

73
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:28,160
Trails and benches that you found, correct?

74
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:29,160
Benches, yes.

75
00:04:29,160 --> 00:04:30,160
Yes.

76
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:35,400
The Concord Bridge, our local newspaper, did a wonderful story that led to a long-lost

77
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:37,480
bench being rediscovered.

78
00:04:37,480 --> 00:04:42,760
But this was something, yes, before this became a story in the Concord Bridge, we had mounted

79
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:49,600
our own campaign to locate all of these bicentennial commemorative granite brentas that the Concord

80
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:51,600
Garden Club had put up.

81
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:56,600
We first encountered one in the cemetery and it was just very strange because it said sort

82
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:02,400
of, it said 1775 to 1975 and we were sort of, what did this bench live for 200 years and

83
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:03,400
then die?

84
00:05:03,400 --> 00:05:04,400
Because there was no context.

85
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:06,400
It just had these dates on it.

86
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:12,720
So then we suddenly saw some others around town and it became a puzzle to figure out

87
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:15,520
where they came from, how many there were.

88
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:20,280
Turns out there are 10, one of which had fallen into the ground but is now being put back

89
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:21,280
up.

90
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:24,560
Well, I have some news for you, you may not know.

91
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:28,440
Part of the initiative of the 250th is that we're going to be some new benches.

92
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:29,440
Really?

93
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:30,440
Yeah.

94
00:05:30,440 --> 00:05:32,440
Oh, I have some thoughts about where that's supposed to go.

95
00:05:32,440 --> 00:05:33,440
Okay, oh, interesting.

96
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:34,440
Yeah.

97
00:05:34,440 --> 00:05:40,000
We connected with the committee and I just learned that very recently so I was like,

98
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,800
oh, I know some people who are going to be very interested in this.

99
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:44,480
Oh, yes.

100
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:46,880
So we've known each other for a while now.

101
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:48,800
How long has it been?

102
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:49,800
It's close to 20 years.

103
00:05:49,800 --> 00:05:50,800
It's getting a lot, yeah.

104
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:51,800
It's close to 20 years.

105
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:52,800
Okay.

106
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:53,800
We have a lot in common.

107
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:54,800
Same birthday.

108
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:55,800
Same birthday, coming up.

109
00:05:55,800 --> 00:05:56,800
Yeah, coming up.

110
00:05:56,800 --> 00:06:03,800
That's A. We're going to go all the way to double Z on December 20th.

111
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:04,800
Yeah.

112
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:07,760
On something we have in common and we're doing this at 3 in the afternoon.

113
00:06:07,760 --> 00:06:08,760
We're enjoying some nice seltzers.

114
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:13,560
I think it might have been more appropriate to do this at about 8 at night with a bourbon.

115
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:15,040
But we have both been sued.

116
00:06:15,040 --> 00:06:16,040
Oh, yes.

117
00:06:16,040 --> 00:06:21,320
Something I want to get into, that's sort of the lead that we'll get to with Deep Springs,

118
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:23,840
but not personally sued.

119
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:27,360
We've both led organizations that have been through lawsuits and anyone who hasn't been

120
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:30,960
through that, I'm sure personally it's even more harrowing.

121
00:06:30,960 --> 00:06:34,960
But even when you're part of an organization and you're just representing an institution

122
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:39,960
and not yourself, it's an experience.

123
00:06:39,960 --> 00:06:43,080
It is life.

124
00:06:43,080 --> 00:06:46,680
It just absolutely upends your life for the duration of the whole thing.

125
00:06:46,680 --> 00:06:51,600
It's kind of wild.

126
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:54,640
Mine was, I remember the first day of business school, I went to the entrepreneurship class.

127
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:59,680
They said, hey, if you start a business, as soon as you get past 30 people in your company,

128
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:03,080
you're just going to start getting sued by people who come out of the woodwork, people

129
00:07:03,080 --> 00:07:04,080
you've never heard of.

130
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:05,280
It's just what happens.

131
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:06,280
It's part of the game.

132
00:07:06,280 --> 00:07:08,000
I thought, that's kind of crazy.

133
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:09,000
There's no way.

134
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,240
Sure enough, it just starts happening.

135
00:07:12,240 --> 00:07:17,080
People you've never heard of just start suing you over weird stuff.

136
00:07:17,080 --> 00:07:23,560
Yours is way more interesting though, because I think even starting with the beginnings

137
00:07:23,560 --> 00:07:27,880
of how you got to Deep Springs all the way through to when you left, it saw some major

138
00:07:27,880 --> 00:07:32,840
change and you helped lead the school to that change.

139
00:07:32,840 --> 00:07:38,160
Maybe we could just start at how you found Deep Springs, what Deep Springs is, and what

140
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:39,880
drew you there in the first place.

141
00:07:39,880 --> 00:07:41,600
Yeah, absolutely.

142
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:46,960
While I was at Concord Academy, my first time here in Concord, I did spend one semester

143
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:52,920
away at what was then a new program called Mountain School, which is in Vermont.

144
00:07:52,920 --> 00:07:59,120
It's a one semester program for high school students that is both... It's a sort of a

145
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:00,120
farm school.

146
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:02,000
It's a school and a farm.

147
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:03,120
It's unusual.

148
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:09,760
It's a very environmentally centered education, but it's also unusual in that it really allows

149
00:08:09,760 --> 00:08:15,960
its students more responsibility than your typical high school would and asks them to

150
00:08:15,960 --> 00:08:23,240
participate meaningfully in a lot of decisions as well as responsibilities in running the

151
00:08:23,240 --> 00:08:24,240
school.

152
00:08:24,240 --> 00:08:31,200
Making sure at that time a lot of the facilities were heated by wood.

153
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:37,120
We would literally have to fell trees and chainsaw them up and chop them up and feed

154
00:08:37,120 --> 00:08:42,040
them to the dragon, which is what we call a giant wood burning stove.

155
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:51,800
It was an incredibly life changing, meaningful experience for me being up there, being with

156
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:59,280
these other students, these other young people who were so interested in sort of jump starting

157
00:08:59,280 --> 00:09:03,120
their career as adults and being treated like adults.

158
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:10,320
Being given those responsibilities and opportunities and also just that connection to living in

159
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:14,360
a place and having a better sense of what it took to actually run the whole school and

160
00:09:14,360 --> 00:09:16,520
being able to see all of that.

161
00:09:16,520 --> 00:09:17,520
I loved it.

162
00:09:17,520 --> 00:09:21,880
It was there that I first heard about another institution called Deep Springs College, which

163
00:09:21,880 --> 00:09:25,360
was college rather than a high school.

164
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:29,680
It was spoken of at the mountain school as being somewhat of an inspiration and a like

165
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:31,840
minded institution.

166
00:09:31,840 --> 00:09:38,120
Deep Springs takes it to another level in terms of the degree of student participation.

167
00:09:38,120 --> 00:09:43,680
The other thing I learned about Deep Springs immediately was that it was only for male

168
00:09:43,680 --> 00:09:47,240
students, that it was not a coeducational school.

169
00:09:47,240 --> 00:09:53,760
There was no possibility I could go and there was no similar school that admitted women.

170
00:09:53,760 --> 00:09:54,760
That was that.

171
00:09:54,760 --> 00:09:56,240
I sort of followed that away.

172
00:09:56,240 --> 00:10:01,800
If that had been available to me, I definitely would have applied.

173
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:05,320
I went to University of Chicago, which ended up being a great place for me in all kinds

174
00:10:05,320 --> 00:10:07,520
of ways.

175
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:12,800
No sour grapes there, but it had definitely stuck with me that I was interested in it.

176
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:20,400
Went on, got my PhD, was doing really intense ecological research that took me.

177
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:25,040
I had five different field sites in Europe that I was visiting from near the Arctic Circle

178
00:10:25,040 --> 00:10:31,200
up in Finland to the Mediterranean coast and everywhere in between.

179
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:32,640
Really all in on the research.

180
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:39,480
Part of that project was I had students working with me at each of my field sites.

181
00:10:39,480 --> 00:10:43,360
Throughout my graduate student career and that research career, a lot of the experience

182
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:48,880
I found most rewarding was really working closely with the students.

183
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:52,600
I was recognizing that education was something important to me.

184
00:10:52,600 --> 00:10:57,760
When I was starting to think about faculty jobs, I was looking at complete opposite ends

185
00:10:57,760 --> 00:10:58,760
of the spectrum.

186
00:10:58,760 --> 00:11:01,800
I was looking at ones that were completely research focused.

187
00:11:01,800 --> 00:11:06,760
Then I was looking at smaller rural arts schools, places that were much more teaching focused.

188
00:11:06,760 --> 00:11:12,720
I thought to myself, hmm, now I know Deep Springs doesn't admit female students, but

189
00:11:12,720 --> 00:11:16,320
I know women work there on the staff and as teachers.

190
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:23,120
I wonder where the heck is going on with Deep Springs and if they might be looking for professors.

191
00:11:23,120 --> 00:11:28,320
I went and looked and sure enough, they were hiring a science professor.

192
00:11:28,320 --> 00:11:33,360
This might be my chance to check out Deep Springs and get there a completely different

193
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:35,080
way.

194
00:11:35,080 --> 00:11:38,080
So I applied.

195
00:11:38,080 --> 00:11:42,600
That was not the normal way, especially for long term professors.

196
00:11:42,600 --> 00:11:49,040
Certainly at that time, a lot of the people who came to Deep Springs as professors had

197
00:11:49,040 --> 00:11:54,800
already been there for the long term professorships, had been there as visitors before or had some

198
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:56,320
direct experience.

199
00:11:56,320 --> 00:11:57,880
I didn't know anyone there.

200
00:11:57,880 --> 00:12:02,040
I was basically cold calling.

201
00:12:02,040 --> 00:12:07,360
I still sort of in a way, I sort of can't believe that I made it onto the radar, but

202
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:13,880
I went out there and it instantly, two things became very clear to me.

203
00:12:13,880 --> 00:12:18,480
One was it was going to be a fascinating place to be a science teacher and to be a science

204
00:12:18,480 --> 00:12:22,720
educator and would be like no other opportunity I would have.

205
00:12:22,720 --> 00:12:26,440
The other one immediately, I did not go into it thinking, hey, I want to make this place

206
00:12:26,440 --> 00:12:27,440
coeducational.

207
00:12:27,440 --> 00:12:28,920
I haven't been into it very much.

208
00:12:28,920 --> 00:12:32,600
I want to see what Deep Springs is like.

209
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:42,000
But I would say very quickly, my experiences there, I shouldn't say instant, but very quickly

210
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:45,920
it became very clear to me that there was something really missing from the students

211
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:53,080
experience because they have so much power and responsibility there.

212
00:12:53,080 --> 00:12:58,840
Something was really missing from their experience in excluding half of the world's population,

213
00:12:58,840 --> 00:13:02,920
especially since the mission of the school is to prepare, at that time it was to prepare

214
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:08,320
young men, now it's to prepare young people, not to jump the shark, but to prepare young

215
00:13:08,320 --> 00:13:11,120
people for a life of service to humanity.

216
00:13:11,120 --> 00:13:17,760
If you're preparing for that and off the bat excluding half of humanity from participating

217
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:22,640
in that work, it's a limited experience.

218
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:23,640
Sure.

219
00:13:23,640 --> 00:13:24,640
Yeah.

220
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:31,760
My understanding is that out there, like you were saying, it's to another level.

221
00:13:31,760 --> 00:13:34,720
You're not just chopping down wood to heat the place.

222
00:13:34,720 --> 00:13:37,120
You want food, you're growing your own food.

223
00:13:37,120 --> 00:13:40,240
You want a steak, go get the steak.

224
00:13:40,240 --> 00:13:41,240
Yeah.

225
00:13:41,240 --> 00:13:42,240
Absolutely.

226
00:13:42,240 --> 00:13:45,240
I used to tell you processed cows?

227
00:13:45,240 --> 00:13:46,240
Yes.

228
00:13:46,240 --> 00:13:47,240
Yes.

229
00:13:47,240 --> 00:13:52,800
One thing I do want to say is Deep Springs is not wholly self-sufficient in terms of

230
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:57,000
the food it produces, but at least when I left, it was wholly self-sufficient in terms

231
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:03,400
of all the meat that it produced and all of the eggs and all of the dairy.

232
00:14:03,400 --> 00:14:08,360
That fluctuates over time depending on the student interests and the staff availability,

233
00:14:08,360 --> 00:14:09,960
but it certainly was when I left.

234
00:14:09,960 --> 00:14:14,560
Well, I remember during COVID, you describing what it was like there.

235
00:14:14,560 --> 00:14:15,560
Yes.

236
00:14:15,560 --> 00:14:19,400
In the country, it seemed like one of the safest places to be in the world because you

237
00:14:19,400 --> 00:14:20,400
had some touch.

238
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:23,920
I remember you saying you did have some suppliers coming in and you had very strict protocols

239
00:14:23,920 --> 00:14:28,240
with what you did when you got deliveries and what you did with the materials, what

240
00:14:28,240 --> 00:14:29,240
they came.

241
00:14:29,240 --> 00:14:30,240
Yeah.

242
00:14:30,240 --> 00:14:32,520
But I mean, this is self-sufficiency has its advantages.

243
00:14:32,520 --> 00:14:33,520
It does.

244
00:14:33,520 --> 00:14:34,520
Yeah.

245
00:14:34,520 --> 00:14:35,520
Yeah.

246
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:36,520
And it's very isolated too.

247
00:14:36,520 --> 00:14:42,080
The school is located in a high mountain valley in Eastern California, almost on the Nevada

248
00:14:42,080 --> 00:14:43,080
border.

249
00:14:43,080 --> 00:14:47,200
The school's at about a mile elevation.

250
00:14:47,200 --> 00:14:48,200
It's in a basin.

251
00:14:48,200 --> 00:14:50,000
There's nothing in the basin but that school.

252
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:55,040
So you have to go over a mountain pass and over an hour in each direction to get to a

253
00:14:55,040 --> 00:14:56,040
town.

254
00:14:56,040 --> 00:15:00,520
And it's not like the nearest towns are particularly bustling and huge.

255
00:15:00,520 --> 00:15:02,160
So it's quite isolated.

256
00:15:02,160 --> 00:15:07,360
But yes, in terms of the meat, yes, Deep Springs is another level.

257
00:15:07,360 --> 00:15:09,520
Yes, I did participate.

258
00:15:09,520 --> 00:15:16,760
I did not actually ever directly kill any of the large animals while I was there, but

259
00:15:16,760 --> 00:15:20,520
I definitely helped break them, what we call break them down.

260
00:15:20,520 --> 00:15:26,000
It's a nice euphemism, but empty out the guts and butcher them.

261
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:29,600
For a cow, that's not something that happens that same day.

262
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:30,600
That's a big task.

263
00:15:30,600 --> 00:15:31,600
That's a huge animal.

264
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:32,600
That's a big task.

265
00:15:32,600 --> 00:15:35,440
That takes some time to do that.

266
00:15:35,440 --> 00:15:37,480
So I definitely helped with that.

267
00:15:37,480 --> 00:15:40,480
That was not an expectation of my job.

268
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:42,360
That is an expectation of the students.

269
00:15:42,360 --> 00:15:43,360
This was really more.

270
00:15:43,360 --> 00:15:46,000
Yeah, but you wanted to understand the whole experience there.

271
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:47,640
I did want to understand the whole experience.

272
00:15:47,640 --> 00:15:51,680
And it was an amazing, this was one of the things that was so fabulous about teaching

273
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:56,000
at Deep Springs was, you know, there were especially, you know, as a scientist, there

274
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:57,520
were all these opportunities.

275
00:15:57,520 --> 00:16:04,040
So I taught a course on vertebrate functional anatomy, which is sort of looking at an animal

276
00:16:04,040 --> 00:16:08,240
and understanding something about its lifestyle from the way that it's built.

277
00:16:08,240 --> 00:16:15,000
So you can tell if an animal is likely eating, has primarily a plant-based diet or primarily

278
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:19,920
a meat-based diet based on its teeth and its stomach and things like that.

279
00:16:19,920 --> 00:16:25,800
So for that course, we went to a cow slaughter and emptied all the guts into a front lobe,

280
00:16:25,800 --> 00:16:28,240
not to get too gross, and then rooted through them.

281
00:16:28,240 --> 00:16:31,760
I think people should know what happens when they're eating a burger.

282
00:16:31,760 --> 00:16:32,760
They should know how it gets there.

283
00:16:32,760 --> 00:16:38,040
Yeah, and the students had to, you know, go through this front loader bucket and identify

284
00:16:38,040 --> 00:16:43,120
everything and see how it was all connected and look on the outside and the inside and

285
00:16:43,120 --> 00:16:44,440
what was in there.

286
00:16:44,440 --> 00:16:45,440
It was extraordinary.

287
00:16:45,440 --> 00:16:49,760
Yeah, it was a really fabulous experience.

288
00:16:49,760 --> 00:16:52,880
And yeah, so there was the meat grown there.

289
00:16:52,880 --> 00:16:59,240
We had a lot of, it was a combination of growing on land, growing meat animals on land that

290
00:16:59,240 --> 00:17:05,680
the college owned and also farmed for hay for the animals, but also grazing on public

291
00:17:05,680 --> 00:17:06,680
land.

292
00:17:06,680 --> 00:17:11,080
So a lot of work I did with the students also was working with all the various government

293
00:17:11,080 --> 00:17:18,040
agencies and some additional organizations that were interested in the environment around

294
00:17:18,040 --> 00:17:27,280
there to really monitor range health and animal health and make sure that, you know, really

295
00:17:27,280 --> 00:17:35,000
understand what it meant to be involved in being stewards of the land in this tradition.

296
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:38,120
So that was, it was really wonderful.

297
00:17:38,120 --> 00:17:43,280
And the students at Deep Springs, you know, they're not just, there's this mystique of

298
00:17:43,280 --> 00:17:46,200
it as the Cowboy College, and that certainly is true.

299
00:17:46,200 --> 00:17:50,960
Every student there has the opportunity to learn how to ride a horse, you know, in the

300
00:17:50,960 --> 00:17:56,480
Western tradition and chase cows on horses and all that, which is something I actually

301
00:17:56,480 --> 00:18:00,360
love to do.

302
00:18:00,360 --> 00:18:08,880
But the students were also, and importantly, involved in writing the application for the

303
00:18:08,880 --> 00:18:11,520
other students and evaluating.

304
00:18:11,520 --> 00:18:14,920
Not only were they involved in that, they headed it.

305
00:18:14,920 --> 00:18:23,680
You know, faculty and staff were available as advisors, but we're not the final deciders

306
00:18:23,680 --> 00:18:24,960
on this, a lot of this.

307
00:18:24,960 --> 00:18:32,680
Yeah, I'd say that I think it's great how much opportunity you give students there for

308
00:18:32,680 --> 00:18:38,400
administrative, leadership, management type tasks.

309
00:18:38,400 --> 00:18:45,160
Because one thing I noticed in my business career, and I think of it is once you become

310
00:18:45,160 --> 00:18:50,200
the person who's in the room leading a meeting, leading discussions, deciding tasks, things

311
00:18:50,200 --> 00:18:52,520
like that, you don't know everything.

312
00:18:52,520 --> 00:18:53,520
You're going to make mistakes.

313
00:18:53,520 --> 00:18:56,360
Like, you're going to mess some stuff up.

314
00:18:56,360 --> 00:19:02,920
And you can immediately tell the people who have never had a leadership position, who

315
00:19:02,920 --> 00:19:10,280
don't empathize, who basically will just attack you if you say one wrong thing or if you,

316
00:19:10,280 --> 00:19:13,880
you know, if you just, I don't know.

317
00:19:13,880 --> 00:19:20,080
I think having at that young of an age responsibility that's meaningful on things that actually

318
00:19:20,080 --> 00:19:24,800
have an effect on the community and to understand what that means, how that feels, what it looks

319
00:19:24,800 --> 00:19:25,800
like.

320
00:19:25,800 --> 00:19:32,520
When I've met students of yours who are in their early 20s, they are some of the most

321
00:19:32,520 --> 00:19:33,760
mature people that I've met.

322
00:19:33,760 --> 00:19:36,800
And it's pretty incredible to me how level headed they are.

323
00:19:36,800 --> 00:19:41,760
And just it's like they are, they seem to have much more experience than the people

324
00:19:41,760 --> 00:19:43,240
of their typical age.

325
00:19:43,240 --> 00:19:44,240
Absolutely.

326
00:19:44,240 --> 00:19:48,720
And one of the experiences I think that Deep Springs really offered them that's valuable

327
00:19:48,720 --> 00:19:55,360
and is not, it's hard to come by for young people these days.

328
00:19:55,360 --> 00:20:01,120
And particularly, I would say, super high achieving young people is the ability to fail.

329
00:20:01,120 --> 00:20:08,040
You know, we, you know, we sort of, we put them in situations that they weren't fully

330
00:20:08,040 --> 00:20:10,720
ready for and asked them to step up to it.

331
00:20:10,720 --> 00:20:17,960
And you know, I don't mean a failure that you can't recover from, but give them that,

332
00:20:17,960 --> 00:20:21,080
I mean that in the positive sense of identifying growth opportunities.

333
00:20:21,080 --> 00:20:22,080
Sure, yeah.

334
00:20:22,080 --> 00:20:25,760
You know, there were, there were people there to help them and to help them work through

335
00:20:25,760 --> 00:20:33,320
it, but they weren't, the safety net was loose, was there, but it was, it was not keeping

336
00:20:33,320 --> 00:20:34,320
them in place.

337
00:20:34,320 --> 00:20:35,920
They could fall into it.

338
00:20:35,920 --> 00:20:40,520
And some of that, you know, and also some of it was, I mean, I was, I was saying Deep

339
00:20:40,520 --> 00:20:47,720
Springs is not totally self-sufficient, but you know, there was no, we had faculty advise,

340
00:20:47,720 --> 00:20:51,840
faculty and staff advisors who were in charge of some of the maintenance work, but the students

341
00:20:51,840 --> 00:20:55,480
did all the cleaning, the dishes, you know.

342
00:20:55,480 --> 00:21:03,760
And so there was a lot of, a lot of emphasis on that as well, that, you know, being a leader,

343
00:21:03,760 --> 00:21:07,640
having a role in the community, taking part in the community is not just a sort of the

344
00:21:07,640 --> 00:21:09,680
glamorous executive tasks.

345
00:21:09,680 --> 00:21:10,680
It's everything.

346
00:21:10,680 --> 00:21:18,720
And I think of, so you know, Congressman Andy Kim is a Deep Springer who most, probably

347
00:21:18,720 --> 00:21:25,120
what most people recognize him for is his, the pictures that were taken of him, the,

348
00:21:25,120 --> 00:21:30,760
on January 7th, the day after the, the Capitol uprising where he was cleaning up trash in

349
00:21:30,760 --> 00:21:31,760
the rotunda.

350
00:21:31,760 --> 00:21:32,760
Yeah.

351
00:21:32,760 --> 00:21:36,320
You know, he showed up early and he just started cleaning up trash and people were so struck

352
00:21:36,320 --> 00:21:39,120
by that, but I saw that and I was just like, there's a Deep Springer.

353
00:21:39,120 --> 00:21:44,440
There's a Deep Springer, you know, he doesn't expect it to be someone else's job, right?

354
00:21:44,440 --> 00:21:51,880
If you see it and it's your, it's in your view, why not do it?

355
00:21:51,880 --> 00:21:59,480
And so, you know, I think that is a really, that's a really important piece of that too.

356
00:21:59,480 --> 00:22:04,280
That it was, it was very gratifying to me that so many, that act resonated with so many

357
00:22:04,280 --> 00:22:08,680
people, but it also to me just seemed like so obvious.

358
00:22:08,680 --> 00:22:12,520
It was not, you know, that he would have done that because that's exactly the kind of thing

359
00:22:12,520 --> 00:22:16,440
that, yeah, that the students at Deep Springs do.

360
00:22:16,440 --> 00:22:17,440
Yeah.

361
00:22:17,440 --> 00:22:18,440
I love that.

362
00:22:18,440 --> 00:22:22,520
So you joined as the science professor, the science faculty.

363
00:22:22,520 --> 00:22:23,520
Yes.

364
00:22:23,520 --> 00:22:24,520
You became dean.

365
00:22:24,520 --> 00:22:26,640
I became dean, yes.

366
00:22:26,640 --> 00:22:32,200
How did, how did the idea of going, I'm sure the idea had been talked about over a long

367
00:22:32,200 --> 00:22:38,400
time there, probably even before you got there, but how did the idea of making the student

368
00:22:38,400 --> 00:22:42,240
body co-ed, how did that come up and how did it actually get turned into action?

369
00:22:42,240 --> 00:22:43,240
Yeah.

370
00:22:43,240 --> 00:22:48,000
So, I mean, it had been discussed literally for decades before I even arrived.

371
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:52,600
And it was a decision that was lay in the hands of the trustees.

372
00:22:52,600 --> 00:23:02,440
So the decision was actually made soon after the trustees took a vote, I think it was 12

373
00:23:02,440 --> 00:23:10,800
in favor and two not in favor of transitioning to co-educational student body just a couple

374
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:15,320
of, within a year or two of my arriving there.

375
00:23:15,320 --> 00:23:20,000
And so that was, that was in the works.

376
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,160
It wasn't entirely my decision.

377
00:23:22,160 --> 00:23:26,420
They did, the trustees did an enormous amount of work sort of studying this and thinking

378
00:23:26,420 --> 00:23:29,480
about what would, what was the history of the school?

379
00:23:29,480 --> 00:23:31,840
What was the intention of the founder?

380
00:23:31,840 --> 00:23:34,780
You know, really trying to honor their work as trustees.

381
00:23:34,780 --> 00:23:41,820
Most of them came to the conclusion that this decision they were making was, would be in

382
00:23:41,820 --> 00:23:48,240
support of what the founder was trying to do, which was primarily establish a special

383
00:23:48,240 --> 00:23:53,400
mode of education, not primarily establish a place for young men.

384
00:23:53,400 --> 00:23:54,400
Right.

385
00:23:54,400 --> 00:23:55,400
Okay.

386
00:23:55,400 --> 00:23:58,440
And, but there were two trustees, both of whom were alums.

387
00:23:58,440 --> 00:24:02,720
They were, you know, there were more alums who voted in favor than not, but two of the

388
00:24:02,720 --> 00:24:06,720
trustees who were both alums voted against it.

389
00:24:06,720 --> 00:24:13,520
So and they were the ones who then sued to stop the school from, from going, becoming

390
00:24:13,520 --> 00:24:14,840
co-educational.

391
00:24:14,840 --> 00:24:21,160
I would say also though, that we did as a faculty, the trustees asked us as a faculty

392
00:24:21,160 --> 00:24:28,320
to write a letter, a position letter to give to them as they considered their decision

393
00:24:28,320 --> 00:24:29,960
what they were going to, how they were going to vote.

394
00:24:29,960 --> 00:24:37,040
And so we, we did, and we laid out our arguments and the faculty was unanimously in favor as

395
00:24:37,040 --> 00:24:42,640
well of transitioning to co-ed, both because we felt like it served the mission of the

396
00:24:42,640 --> 00:24:47,960
school and would be beneficial, directly beneficial to the education of the students to be in

397
00:24:47,960 --> 00:24:56,000
the company of, of a more diverse peer group, but also because we felt it would elevate

398
00:24:56,000 --> 00:25:00,920
the ability of the school to attract and retain good faculty.

399
00:25:00,920 --> 00:25:10,240
I mean, I have to say I got a lot of, there were, there were plenty of people who couldn't

400
00:25:10,240 --> 00:25:12,160
believe that I was teaching at an all male school.

401
00:25:12,160 --> 00:25:13,160
Right.

402
00:25:13,160 --> 00:25:14,160
Right.

403
00:25:14,160 --> 00:25:15,640
Many of my, many of my friends that were saying, what, what are you doing?

404
00:25:15,640 --> 00:25:16,640
Right.

405
00:25:16,640 --> 00:25:18,440
You know, what are you doing at this regressive place?

406
00:25:18,440 --> 00:25:19,440
Yeah.

407
00:25:19,440 --> 00:25:21,200
And that's, but it's, yeah.

408
00:25:21,200 --> 00:25:28,160
But you've, even before you were co-educational, you were still, even in a small student body,

409
00:25:28,160 --> 00:25:34,360
bringing in people from all over the world and who went on to highly successful outcomes.

410
00:25:34,360 --> 00:25:35,360
Absolutely.

411
00:25:35,360 --> 00:25:40,640
So, I mean, it's, it's, it's prestigious before it's co-ed already.

412
00:25:40,640 --> 00:25:41,640
Yes.

413
00:25:41,640 --> 00:25:46,760
I just think it gets better as you add to the applicant pool, to the student body.

414
00:25:46,760 --> 00:25:47,760
Absolutely.

415
00:25:47,760 --> 00:25:52,000
Yeah, and I definitely don't want to denigrate the experience of any of the students who

416
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,320
went through when I was an all male student body.

417
00:25:54,320 --> 00:25:59,080
I, you know, Andy Kim, there's so many, so many of the students, you know, my former

418
00:25:59,080 --> 00:26:02,160
students that you've met were there while it was still all male.

419
00:26:02,160 --> 00:26:03,160
They're, they're extraordinary.

420
00:26:03,160 --> 00:26:07,760
I think they had an extraordinary experience, but the feeling was definitely that it could

421
00:26:07,760 --> 00:26:08,760
get even better.

422
00:26:08,760 --> 00:26:09,760
Yeah, places evolve.

423
00:26:09,760 --> 00:26:10,760
I mean.

424
00:26:10,760 --> 00:26:15,080
So, so that was, that was, that was in the works.

425
00:26:15,080 --> 00:26:25,920
And then, so once the trustees sued to stop the school going co-ed, it then began a very

426
00:26:25,920 --> 00:26:26,920
long legal process.

427
00:26:26,920 --> 00:26:30,880
I don't think any of us realized how long it was going to be.

428
00:26:30,880 --> 00:26:31,880
Right.

429
00:26:31,880 --> 00:26:37,680
At the outset, it ended up going on for, I think, over six years in the end.

430
00:26:37,680 --> 00:26:44,080
But we, it was an unusual, and I'm not a lawyer, so I can't speak to the details completely

431
00:26:44,080 --> 00:26:48,160
accurately of it, but I can speak to my experience of it.

432
00:26:48,160 --> 00:26:55,320
We got a really wonderful, really wonderful local judge was going to be the person hearing

433
00:26:55,320 --> 00:27:01,120
the arguments because what was being the grounds for suing was there, some of the trustees

434
00:27:01,120 --> 00:27:05,940
were trying to change the deed of trust, which was what, where this language of promising

435
00:27:05,940 --> 00:27:07,880
young men came from.

436
00:27:07,880 --> 00:27:11,960
So it was under this special trust law, which I think is much more conservative than a lot

437
00:27:11,960 --> 00:27:20,080
of corporate changing laws of corporations, which is what most universities, what most

438
00:27:20,080 --> 00:27:23,880
universities are so incorporated under.

439
00:27:23,880 --> 00:27:27,040
So it was a different kind of case.

440
00:27:27,040 --> 00:27:33,360
So the judge suggested that the lawsuit be tried in sort of three parts, the first of

441
00:27:33,360 --> 00:27:42,760
which was, could the deed of trust be read as allowing interpretation of that language

442
00:27:42,760 --> 00:27:45,760
to include people who are not men?

443
00:27:45,760 --> 00:27:51,400
And then after that, he wanted us, if that one didn't succeed, then he wanted us to bring

444
00:27:51,400 --> 00:27:57,440
forward a lawsuit saying, you know, would the purpose of the trust be served better

445
00:27:57,440 --> 00:28:00,080
by allowing this change?

446
00:28:00,080 --> 00:28:01,080
Right.

447
00:28:01,080 --> 00:28:07,600
So the first trial was about whether the language could allow for that interpretation, that

448
00:28:07,600 --> 00:28:10,440
you could admit women under the language of the trust.

449
00:28:10,440 --> 00:28:17,880
And I think the non-descending trustees, so I'm just going to call the non-descending

450
00:28:17,880 --> 00:28:23,000
trustees the college, but let's be clear, that's who we're talking about, really thought

451
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:24,360
that was going to go through.

452
00:28:24,360 --> 00:28:26,520
They thought it was not going to be a difficulty.

453
00:28:26,520 --> 00:28:35,120
But the judge disagreed and, you know, I think he was just trying, I don't know what went

454
00:28:35,120 --> 00:28:37,160
on in his mind, but I think he was trying to be very careful.

455
00:28:37,160 --> 00:28:38,160
Right.

456
00:28:38,160 --> 00:28:42,160
And he was being very careful and strategic in the way he suggested this go forward.

457
00:28:42,160 --> 00:28:46,120
But the experience of this was that it was extremely painful because we had thought this

458
00:28:46,120 --> 00:28:48,320
was going to go through.

459
00:28:48,320 --> 00:28:55,760
So we had gone ahead and started to solicit applications from young women as well as young

460
00:28:55,760 --> 00:28:58,560
women.

461
00:28:58,560 --> 00:29:04,320
We had even started to, so the application happens in two rounds, we had even started

462
00:29:04,320 --> 00:29:09,520
to, I think, notify some of the young women that their application was progressing to

463
00:29:09,520 --> 00:29:11,360
the second round.

464
00:29:11,360 --> 00:29:15,520
And then that decision came in and not only that, but there was an injunction against

465
00:29:15,520 --> 00:29:18,120
bringing any young women students on campus.

466
00:29:18,120 --> 00:29:23,720
So it was extremely painful for a lot of people.

467
00:29:23,720 --> 00:29:32,160
And we had to notify all of these young women that they were no longer invited to be part

468
00:29:32,160 --> 00:29:33,160
of the application process.

469
00:29:33,160 --> 00:29:34,160
That stinks.

470
00:29:34,160 --> 00:29:43,800
And again, this was a communication that was, in the end, sent out by the students.

471
00:29:43,800 --> 00:29:50,680
I mean, we helped them with it, we put it together, but it was heartbreaking.

472
00:29:50,680 --> 00:29:54,160
It was very different from what we had hoped for.

473
00:29:54,160 --> 00:29:57,160
If you imagine the first drafts were very different from the final draft.

474
00:29:57,160 --> 00:29:58,160
Yeah.

475
00:29:58,160 --> 00:30:03,920
And there were a lot of drafts that never made it to paper.

476
00:30:03,920 --> 00:30:05,960
So that was very difficult.

477
00:30:05,960 --> 00:30:14,040
But then there was the next stage, which was trying to argue that the purpose of the trust

478
